<p>Spain said it would receive the Dutch-flagged expedition cruise ship MV Hondius in the Canary Islands after a suspected hantavirus outbreak killed three passengers and left others ill while the vessel was off Cape Verde. The World Health Organization said there were 147 passengers and crew on board from 23 nationalities, with seven confirmed or suspected cases, including two laboratory-confirmed infections. Cape Verdean authorities had denied the ship entry to their ports, and Spanish officials acted after coordination with the WHO, which asked Spain to assist those aboard. The WHO said rare human-to-human transmission may have occurred among very close contacts aboard the ship, though hantavirus is usually acquired through exposure to infected rodents or their urine, saliva or droppings. The agency and the European Centre for Disease Prevention and Control said the risk to the wider public was low or very low. Spanish authorities said the ship was expected to reach the Canary Islands in three to four days, where passengers and crew would be examined, treated if needed and repatriated under procedures designed to avoid contact with the local population. Oceanwide Expeditions, the ship’s Dutch operator, said two seriously ill crew members would be evacuated via Cape Verde to the Netherlands before the vessel sailed onward.</p><img src='https://kagiproxy.com/img/-bquzK_Mbsb5gQcH6vqP69NSLffPH15HdD4-uBOeZLeCdQSHs8Ce6mAt996sKLy3ViZ71ooInnuOIBhCXu3wsY8GuB-AlfIjj8GLkH6tr4gINViID6kXAUyAdRhmreIzdxnbEdQsj_bALPbtqoa5oq1ip-enBIkVKkzWtGhnqsH_cqA3QtqU0xxNUwqzqrlqY2R4r4Q2PoxK2gX4_REbGD0I-DSPEdopXOhUA_e1POXTgLEYuelpUbBr97t5_mDYNFmIOx9dMTsL50dLsA' alt='An aerial view of the MV Hondius Dutch cruise ship anchored in the Atlantic off Cabo Verde on Tuesday.' /><br /><h3>Highlights:</h3><ul><li>Medical evacuations: Spain and the WHO arranged for epidemiologists to inspect the vessel; plans also included evacuating two symptomatic passengers to the Netherlands and a high-risk contact to Germany.</li><li>Contact tracing: The WHO began tracing more than 80 passengers from a Saint Helena-to-Johannesburg flight taken by a Dutch passenger whose condition worsened in transit before she died.</li><li>Route correction: The Falkland Islands Government said the Hondius did not visit the islands on the current voyage and that its most recent Falklands call was in mid-February.</li><li>Passenger mix: Oceanwide Expeditions previously listed 88 passengers and 61 crew, including passengers from Britain, the United States, Spain and the Netherlands, and 38 Filipino crew members.</li></ul><h3>Perspectives:</h3><ul><li>Spain’s Health Ministry: The ministry said Spain would receive the ship in the Canary Islands, with contact handled in specially prepared areas and vehicles to protect medical staff and prevent contact with the local population. (<a href='https://www.bbc.com/news/articles/ce8pypvjx1ko'>BBC</a>)</li><li>World Health Organization: A WHO official told CBS News that passengers were in good spirits but could face an eight-week quarantine while authorities assessed transmission risk and medical needs. (<a href='https://www.cbsnews.com/news/spain-agrees-welcome-hantavirus-cruise-ship-who/'>CBS News</a>)</li><li>Cape Verdean authorities: Authorities barred the vessel from docking while health officials worked with international agencies to identify a port able to receive the ship and manage cases. (<a href='https://www.lemonde.fr/en/health/article/2026/05/05/who-tracing-over-80-passengers-on-flight-taken-by-hantavirus-victim_6753163_14.html'>Le Monde</a>)</li><li>Falkland Islands Government: Officials monitored the outbreak because the Hondius frequently operates in Falklands waters, but corrected reports that placed the ship in the islands during the current voyage. (<a href='https://en.mercopress.com/2026/05/05/falklands-monitor-mv-hondius-hantavirus-outbreak-and-clarify-the-route-did-not-include-the-islands'>MercoPress</a>)</li></ul><h3>Sources:</h3><ul>